By using the pt_supervoxel_id as a primary anchor, it was possible to coregister 872 neurons from the imaging session to their specific physical locations in the electron microscopy volume. A major roadblock appeared when the initial database query returned 831 "Unknown" visual area labels, which threatened to make the data unusable for area-specific analysis. To fix this, a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) algorithm was implemented to spatially rescue those missing labels by using the 41 known neurons as anchors to predict the identities of their neighbors.
Stimulus decoding was performed on subpopulations of neurons partitioned by visual area and imaging depth to assess how stimulus information is distributed across the visual hierarchy. 
Across visual areas, RL showed the highest decoding accuracy (0.669 ± 0.053, n=256 neurons), followed by V1 (0.617 ± 0.036, n=378 neurons), and AL (0.536 ± 0.056, n=51 neurons). The relatively poor performance of AL should be interpreted cautiously given its small neuron count (n=51), which limits both statistical power and PCA quality. The strong performance of RL is notable as it is a higher-order visual area, consistent with the hypothesis that areas further along the visual hierarchy carry more abstract or category-level stimulus representations.
Across imaging depths, 220 μm showed the highest decoding accuracy (0.614 ± 0.051, n=314 neurons), which corresponds approximately to cortical layer 4 — the primary thalamic input layer and a major site of feedforward visual processing. Superficial neurons at 80 μm performed worst (0.456 ± 0.038, n=64 neurons), though this subpopulation was also the smallest. Deeper layers (360 and 500 μm) showed intermediate and comparable performance (0.578 and 0.588 respectively), suggesting sustained stimulus encoding across deeper cortical layers.
In the combined area × depth analysis, RL@220um achieved the highest accuracy among all subpopulations (0.599 ± 0.073, n=156), while RL@80um performed the worst within RL (0.406 ± 0.025, n=16). V1 showed relatively consistent decoding across depths (0.502–0.583), suggesting a distributed rather than layer-specific encoding strategy in primary visual cortex. AL subpopulations were too sparse at most depths to draw strong conclusions, with AL@500um performing near chance (0.461 ± 0.071, n=14).
